      "text": "To determine the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) in degrees, we can use the properties of triangles and the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nGiven the triangle \\(ABD\\) where \\(\\angle ABD = 130^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle BAD = 93^\\circ\\), we can find \\(\\angle BDA\\) as follows:\n\n1. The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\angle ABD + \\angle BAD + \\angle BDA = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n2. Substituting the given values, we get:\n   \\[\n   130^\\circ + 93^\\circ + \\angle BDA = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n3. Simplifying this equation:\n   \\[\n   223^\\circ + \\angle BDA = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n4. Solving for \\(\\angle BDA\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle BDA = 180^\\circ - 223^\\circ = 37^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n5. Now, we know \\(\\angle BDA\\) and \\(\\angle DBC\\) are supplementary (they add up to \\(180^\\circ\\)).\n\n6. In the right triangle \\(BCD\\), we have:\n   - \\(\\angle DBC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BDA = 180^\\circ - 37^\\circ = 143^\\circ\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle BCD = \\angle BCA = 47^\\circ\\) (since \\(\\angle DBC\\) is part of an entire \\(180^\\circ\\)).\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{47}\n\\]",
